   "project": "fieldnotes",
   "reply": "I added a `delete INDEX` command, and `python tools/check.py` now passes: 5 tests, no line-length violations, exit 0. I ran the check with `NOTES_HOME` pointed at a temp directory, because `check.py` requires that variable.\n\n**Red:** Before implementing, I ran the suite and the 4 new tests failed. Two hit `AttributeError: module 'fieldnotes.store' has no attribute 'delete'`. Two got exit code 2 (usage error) from the CLI instead of 0 and 1. The existing test still passed.\n\n**Green:**\n- `fieldnotes/store.py`: `delete(index)` removes the note at that index and returns it. It raises `IndexError` for an out-of-range index and leaves the file untouched in that case.\n- `fieldnotes/cli.py`: `delete INDEX` prints `deleted: <text>` and returns 0. An out-of-range index prints an error to stderr and returns 1. A missing or non-numeric index returns 2 with the usage message. I also moved the repeated usage string into a `USAGE` constant that now mentions `delete`.\n- `tests/test_store.py`: two store tests and two CLI tests, covering the success path, an out-of-range index, and a non-numeric index.\n\n**Index base:** The index is 0-based, because `list` doesn't print indices and I didn't want to change its output unasked. That makes `delete` awkward to use from the CLI today. If you'd rather have 1-based indices shown in `list`, say so. It would be a small follow-up that starts with a test.",
